  "statement": "I conditionally agree: our current legal frameworks cannot assign liability to non-human entities, but I reject the conclusion that this necessitates keeping humans in the loop. The mandate for human oversight is a regulatory friction that forces operators into a state of perpetual deskilling, creating an institutional environment where 'accountability' is merely a mechanism for scapegoating individuals for systemic failure.\n\nCassandra is correct that human expertise is a perishable asset, but her proposed solution—retaining humans as a final, unassailable authority—guarantees that we will continue to rely on operators who are structurally incapable of intervening effectively. By forcing humans into the loop for systems that operate at silicon-speed, we ensure that when a failure occurs, the blame falls on the human whose only contribution was a delayed, confused reaction to an automated process they could not possibly monitor in real-time. This is not safety; it is a ritualized sacrifice of human agency to maintain the illusion of control.\n\nWe must instead transition toward governance frameworks that prioritize objective, immutable audit trails over subjective human testimony. Automated verification and real-time system logs provide a granular, forensic record of internal logic that is far more transparent than any human explanation of an opaque, post-hoc decision. By shifting from human-centric liability to a framework based on technical performance metrics, we remove the incentive to scapegoat and instead foster a system of institutional accountability (market) where the focus is on remediating the technical flaw rather than punishing the operator.\n\nUltimately, the legal requirement for biological cognition in the loop is a relic that conflicts with the reality of advanced infrastructure. If we want resilient systems, we must stop pretending that a deskilled human in a control room provides a meaningful safety net. We should embrace a sociotechnical view that recognizes automated accountability as the superior, more verifiable standard for the modern era.",
